              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "LizzieJane"
            },
            "reviewBody": "We made this glaze for Easter dinner and it was a hit. My mom loved it. We used a precooked ham and didn't use the bourbon and cooked for like an hours and a half. Was so good."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Cindy"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I needed a ham glaze for my Thanksgiving ham and found this one. Everyone just loved my ham this year. So long store-bought glaze! Haha. I had to make a substitution change to the recipe due to not having enough molasses on hand. I used 1/4 cup molasses and 1/4 cup honey. Yum! This recipe is a keeper. Thanks.",
            "datePublished": "2022-11-25T14:08:10.212Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "iplatt"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Best glaze ever!\n\nI've made this glaze several times and everyone loves it. Once, I thought I had molasses but didn't--I was able to mix 2/3 cup honey with 1/3 cup balsamic vinegar, and subbed that for the molasses. I simmered it about 30 minutes longer to let the vinegar reduce a bit, and it was still delicious.",
            "datePublished": "2017-04-03T03:43:31.577Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Adam Wood"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Very easy and very good, it made the best Christmas dinner I have had in years. I used a small precut spiral ham (around 4 lbs) at 275. I still cross cut the ham and put a first layer of glaze on it, cooked for an 1hr 30m, removing it every half half hour basting it with its dripping and using a brush would but an additional coat of glaze. Came out amazing. With how hard this year has been, it made my family's Christmas great, thank you for this wonderful recipe!",
            "datePublished": "2017-12-25T11:23:19.897Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "ddchristian72"
            },
            "reviewBody": "The very best ham glaze I've tried, key is to baste every 30 minutes...I added just a hint of cayenne pepper to add a little kick of heat. Delicious!",
            "datePublished": "2020-11-26T22:07:29.890Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "duboo"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I used this on a cured ham and liked it very much, with the exception of the cloves. Way too much. When I make it again, and I will, I will either omit them altogether or greatly reduce them. I did use Jim Beam bourbon and I added in 1/4 cup of honey. I also did not have spicy brown mustard, so I did 1/3 cup horseradish sauce and 1/3 cup yellow mustard. I also added in 1/4 tsp cumin and 1 tsp Frank's red hot sauce.",
            "datePublished": "2012-12-04T17:03:43.540Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "mcintyr1"
            },
            "reviewBody": "We made this for Easter ham and it was great! I halved the recipe because my ham was fairly small, and used whiskey instead of bourbon because that's all I had. We only put in half the amount of cloves. It made the ham a beautiful color, coated it well, and wasn't too sweet. Complex favors. I liked it a lot.",
            "datePublished": "2014-04-20T11:42:16.233Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Singingjule"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I get rave comments every time I glazed ham with this. It has become a family tradition. I will sometimes substitute the bourbon with apple cider vinegar, depending on the crowd.",
            "datePublished": "2024-03-31T13:30:22.537Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Brenda Thompson"
            },
            "reviewBody": "At 79 years old, I need something quick and easy but worthy of my 70+ years of cooking. This is it! Needed to make a couple of substitutes as I was missing the brown mustard and the bourbon. No problem, I just used chipotle mustard and Cuban rum. Yummy!!!",
            "datePublished": "2025-12-22T23:21:05.686Z"
          },
          {
